@@ -0,0 +1,94 @@
+# LongSpark
+
+LongSpark predicts seven draft tokens in parallel and applies a sequential
+Markov correction before greedy sampling. It summarizes confirmed target KV
+into 16 global memory slots and retains 256 recent target rows. The scheduler,
+target forward pass, rejection sampler, and request lifecycle remain owned by
+vLLM's Model Runner V2.
+
+The initial implementation supports dense Qwen3 targets in BF16 on a single
+CUDA GPU, with FlashAttention and a compatible Global16/Raw256/Local7 checkpoint.
+It shares the target embedding, output head, and final normalization weights.
+Checkpoint parameter names are compatible with the original
+[LongSpark implementation](https://github.com/dawncc/LongSpark).
+
+## Serve an OpenAI-compatible API
+
+```bash
+VLLM_USE_V2_MODEL_RUNNER=1 vllm serve Qwen/Qwen3-4B \
+  --served-model-name longspark-qwen3-4b \
+  --dtype bfloat16 \
+  --no-enable-prefix-caching \
+  --max-model-len 4096 \
+  --max-num-seqs 4 \
+  --max-num-batched-tokens 512 \
+  --compilation-config '{"cudagraph_mode":"FULL_AND_PIECEWISE"}' \
+  --speculative-config '{"method":"longspark","model":"Hehy/LongSpark-Qwen3-4B","num_speculative_tokens":7}'
+```
+
+Use the normal completions or chat completions endpoints, including streaming.
+Set `temperature` explicitly to zero:
+
+```python
+from openai import OpenAI
+
+client = OpenAI(base_url="http://localhost:8000/v1", api_key="EMPTY")
+response = client.chat.completions.create(
+    model="longspark-qwen3-4b",
+    messages=[{"role": "user", "content": "What is 19 times 23?"}],
+    temperature=0,
+    max_tokens=256,
+    extra_body={"chat_template_kwargs": {"enable_thinking": False}},
+)
+print(response.choices[0].message.content)
+```
+
+## Offline generation
+
+The same configuration is accepted by `LLM`. Set
+`VLLM_USE_V2_MODEL_RUNNER=1` in the environment before starting Python:
+
+```python
+from vllm import LLM, SamplingParams
+
+llm = LLM(
+    model="Qwen/Qwen3-4B",
+    dtype="bfloat16",
+    enable_prefix_caching=False,
+    max_model_len=4096,
+    max_num_seqs=4,
+    max_num_batched_tokens=512,
+    speculative_config={
+        "method": "longspark",
+        "model": "Hehy/LongSpark-Qwen3-4B",
+        "num_speculative_tokens": 7,
+    },
+)
+outputs = llm.generate(["The capital of France is"], SamplingParams(temperature=0))
+print(outputs[0].outputs[0].text)
+```
+
+## Supported configuration
+
+- Model Runner V2; seven fixed draft tokens; greedy target and draft sampling.
+- Dense Qwen3, matching target/draft vocabulary and geometry, native unscaled RoPE.
+- BF16 target weights and KV cache; FlashAttention is selected for this method.
+- One device: TP, PP, DP, DCP, and PCP must all be one.
+- Chunked prefill, mixed prefill/decode batches, request-slot reuse, asynchronous
+  scheduling, and native CUDA graph capture for the draft network and sampling.
+- `--enforce-eager` is available for debugging. Full decode graphs are recommended
+  for latency measurements.
+
+Prefix caching must be disabled because the drafter needs to observe every
+confirmed prompt row. LoRA, KV transfer, quantization, watermarking, adaptive
+verification, structured output, custom logits processors, sampling penalties,
+and nonzero temperature are not supported by this initial implementation.
+Unsupported requests are rejected before reaching the worker.
+Streaming input (incrementally appending prompt chunks through `AsyncLLM`) is
+not supported; ordinary streaming output through the HTTP API is supported.
+
+Draft state and prefill workspace add GPU memory overhead. Increase the batch
+and token budgets gradually from the example above. Throughput and acceptance
+depend on the target/checkpoint pair, prompt distribution, and batch size; use
+the standard vLLM benchmarking tools to measure your workload. The usual
+[speculative decoding acceptance metrics](acceptance_metrics.md) apply.
